WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.018 --> 00:00:03.878
(soft thudding)

00:00:03.878 --> 00:00:07.045
(snow crunching)

00:00:10.406 --> 00:00:12.360
- Welcome everyone. As you can see,

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.690
there's a lot of snow in Germany.

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:16.410
This is really a condition that is

00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.040
pretty much unparalleled for years.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.430
There hasn't been that
much snow here in Germany.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:24.030
So welcome to another
episode of "Firm Learning."

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.100
My name is Heinrich and on my channel

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.860
I want to help you to be
successful in the first years

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:30.810
of your career.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:33.030
Today, we are going to
talk about a question

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.370
that is really crucial

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:37.560
for every professional services context.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.720
Doesn't matter if you
wanna talk with colleagues,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.350
with clients, the question often is:

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.380
How can you be trusted?

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.770
How do you build trust
with your clients and

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:49.050
with your coworkers?

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.550
And there is one concept, one framework

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.430
that is frequently taught in consulting,

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.470
onboarding programs, in training programs

00:00:57.470 --> 00:01:01.277
of MBB consulting firms like
McKinsey, BCG, and Bain.

00:01:01.277 --> 00:01:04.430
And this is the so-called
"trust equation."

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.020
The trust equation.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.330
This is what we're going
to talk about today.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:09.947
I'm going to explain to you

00:01:09.947 --> 00:01:12.720
what the trust equation is all about,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.180
but don't worry I will not
keep it purely theoretical,

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.040
but I will give you very tangible advice

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.810
on how you can translate
that to your daily work,

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.920
on how you can build trust
in your work environment.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.660
And again, it doesn't really matter

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.630
whether it's consulting, industry, banking

00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:34.690
trust this will be
applicable for many contexts.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:38.490
So stick in here with me
and let's get started.

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:40.120
So what is the trust equation?

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:41.890
To my knowledge, the trust equation

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.770
was first described by David Maister

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:46.930
in his book, "The Trusted Advisor."

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:48.880
It shows a basic relationship

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.320
what the factors are that contribute

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.100
to you building trust with another person

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.210
and more specifically

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:57.690
that the other person actually trusts you.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:58.990
And according to this equation

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.220
there are three factors
that increase trust

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:03.770
but there's also one
factor to reduce trust.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:05.680
So let's briefly go over these factors

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.800
but then later we'll also
provide very tangible examples

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.880
on how to make this actionable to you

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.020
on what you can do to actually build

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.370
and increase trust
according to this framework.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:17.300
So let's go one by one, credibility.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.960
This is about the knowledge,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.680
are you perceived as
somebody who is credible,

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.330
who knows his or her stuff

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:24.960
who really knows what he's talking about.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.640
Second, reliability is
more about your actions.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:28.910
Can you be trusted

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:31.520
that you will actually do
what you promise to do?

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:33.940
Will you deliver, are you reliable?

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:36.360
Third, intimacy, and
this is about building

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.570
a personal relationship, a
bond with the other person.

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:42.980
Does the other person feel
like he or she is close to you?

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.420
Of course, a bond helps to
build and develop trust.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.700
And then the fourth
factor, self-orientation

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.910
is rather something that reduces trust.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.470
It will hurt you. It will
undermine the relationship

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:54.990
that you are building with a person.

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.220
Are you perceived as someone

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:58.220
who only thinks about him or herself?

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.030
Can you be trusted that you also

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.830
have the other person in mind

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.920
or will you do things only to the extent

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:05.910
that they actually benefit you directly?

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.730
This is the trust equation and understand

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.800
if you not say, hey,
that's a bit theoretic.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.360
What does this really mean for me?

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.950
How can I really put this
to life to build trust?

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:17.640
And this is what we
will dig into right now.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.530
And the first factor is credibility.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.240
And this is, indeed, maybe
the most obvious one.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.900
Of course you want to be
perceived as credible.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.180
You want to be perceived
as a domain expert

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.820
as a knowledgeable practitioner.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.680
And so in industries like consulting

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.100
especially for the most junior colleagues

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.800
this can of course be an issue

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:37.100
because often you will be a young graduate

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.850
fresh out of university.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.070
How will you be the domain expert

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:44.310
for a certain topic, of a
project that you are staffed on?

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:45.920
So there are a couple of things that I did

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:47.760
when I was staffed as a young colleague

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.450
on a project where maybe I
didn't feel too comfortable

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:52.010
about the topic that was being covered.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.720
So the first is fact packs.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:55.360
In many professional services firms

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:56.980
you will have access to knowledge,

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.270
to databases, to industry reports.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.110
Download lots of these, read them

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.780
potentially also in your
free time on the weekend.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:06.650
Make sure that you have
a solid understanding

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:08.760
of all the challenges that
the industry is facing.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.110
And, last but not least,
ask your leadership,

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:14.410
the responsible partners,
to provide a little talk

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.100
in the first week of the project.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.540
Often the senior partners
already covered the client

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.270
for several years, they really know

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:21.980
the organization inside out

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.110
and sometimes they will do
this on their own initiative

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.790
but often it also kind of
help just to ask them for it.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.650
And then in most cases, they will be happy

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:30.140
to just spend one or two hours

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:31.840
giving you a download of everything

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:33.670
that they know about the client,

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:35.800
about the topics that are top of mind

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:37.880
of the board members of that client.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.230
And of course this can
be tremendously helpful

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:41.400
for your engagement.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.490
The second element of the
trust formula is reliability.

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:46.580
What can you do so that the client

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.010
perceives you as reliable?

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:51.270
And I remember my very
first project at McKinsey

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.360
where I got a little
tip from a project lead,

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.650
and she told me "Heinrich,
it's actually pretty easy

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:58.370
just on purpose put yourself in situations

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:01.360
where you can prove to the
client that you are reliable."

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.370
So next time you sit
together with the client,

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:06.240
tell him or her, okay, I will
send this document to you

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.970
until tomorrow 10:00 A.M,

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:11.130
or say, okay, today at
7:00 P.M in the evening,

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.610
I will give you a call.

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:15.820
So make these little commitments,
these little promises

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:18.760
and then of course, make sure
that you really fulfill them.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.630
If you commit to give a call at seven

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:24.660
don't call at five past or 10
past seven, but call at seven.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:26.360
If you promise to send the document

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:28.120
until next day 10:00 A.M,

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.320
do that and don't send it at 11:00 A.M.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:31.850
It is often these little things

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:33.470
that will help you to be perceived

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:36.440
as reliable and trust enough
from my current experiences,

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:39.080
working as a CTO in a
German retail company.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.677
It happens to me every single
day that people tell me

00:05:42.677 --> 00:05:44.780
I will call you tomorrow at this time,

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.850
I will send you this at this time

00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:48.640
and then people don't do that.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:52.170
This really happens every
day and it drives me crazy

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:54.330
and, of course, this is exactly what leads

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:55.440
to these impressions that

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.260
I do not really trust these people,

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:00.020
that I do not find these
people to be reliable.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:03.110
So don't be like that, but
prove on a regular basis

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:05.270
that you are reliable and this, indeed,

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:06.990
will lead to trust.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:08.970
The next part of the trust equation

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.800
I wanna talk about today is intimacy.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:15.530
How do you build intimacy with
colleagues, with coworkers,

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:17.780
and we have two tips for you to share.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.670
And the first is a tip that I got

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.140
from a former project
lead that I worked with.

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:23.950
And he told me, well, Heinrich,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.020
what you can do is whenever you visit

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:28.610
a client in his office, or now today,

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:31.390
even in a video call, if
you are hanging out on Zoom,

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:34.071
on Teams or whatever,
just look at the things

00:06:34.071 --> 00:06:36.830
that are in the office of this person

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:38.790
and ask him or her about that.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.580
Wow, that's an interesting picture.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:42.090
That's an interesting little statue.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:43.270
What is it all about?

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:47.100
This is great to build some
intimacy with your client

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.480
because this often will spark
some very personal stories,

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:53.350
very personal things that you
will learn from that person.

00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:55.440
And the second tip to build intimacy

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.760
is really just to share some
personal stories of yours.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:00.826
Talk about what you do in your free time.

00:07:00.826 --> 00:07:03.460
Talk about some hobbies of yours.

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:07.300
Let the other person know
you, open up a little bit.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:11.150
This, again, will build some
relationships, some bonding.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:13.540
And of course this often also encourages

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:16.510
the other person to share
something personal as well.

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:18.670
And this is then often the starting point

00:07:18.670 --> 00:07:21.620
of a deeper, more intimate relationship

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:24.410
with this colleague or client
that you wanna bond with.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:27.100
And now the last factor
is self-orientation.

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:28.420
And, again, this is something

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:30.490
that you don't wanna be perceived as.

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:32.050
This is something that will hurt

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:33.787
the trust relationship
that you wanna build.

00:07:33.787 --> 00:07:35.730
And this is, indeed, a tricky thing

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:37.630
in many professional services context,

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:39.830
because, of course, the
majority of the people

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:42.890
on the client side that you
work with on an everyday basis,

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:45.270
this is not really the
people that you work for

00:07:45.270 --> 00:07:47.860
but you need to work with them
to get something from them

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:49.780
to get some inputs, to get data.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.860
You know, from the
perspective of these people

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:54.180
it's very easy for them to feel used.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:56.650
They will often and
easily get the perception

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:59.220
that the whole relationship
is a one way street,

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:01.030
that you want to have something from them,

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:03.510
but you do not really care
at all about these guys.

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:06.140
You do not care at all
how he or she is doing,

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:08.030
whether what you are doing is contributing

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:09.220
in any way to her work.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:11.670
And of course, sometimes
this is hard to avoid

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:13.990
but I found it always very worthwhile,

00:08:13.990 --> 00:08:16.010
especially with those
people where you know

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:18.240
that these will be key
contact people for you,

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:20.390
where you know that your project success

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:22.260
will really depend on the relationship

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:24.320
that you will be able to
build with that person.

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.460
Make it a point to always also give back.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:30.600
Do things for this person that
maybe you don't need to do.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:31.910
Ask this person at the beginning,

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:33.440
hey, what are you working on?

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:35.130
Is there anything I can help with?

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:36.360
Maybe you can help this person

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.640
with an analysis that
this person is doing.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:41.120
Try to do a little favor for this person.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:43.870
Another problem that often
arises in these contexts

00:08:43.870 --> 00:08:45.700
is that especially as a young consultant

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:47.850
you feel like that you
want to put yourself

00:08:47.850 --> 00:08:49.200
into the center of attention.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:50.890
So maybe you were co-creating

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:52.740
a certain result with a client.

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:54.090
Now you want to present something

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:56.800
in front of a senior leader
of that organization.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.450
And here it can often be so
powerful for the relationship

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:01.290
to pre-discuss this with the client

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.900
and let the client do
the whole presentation.

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:05.930
Let's make the client the winner here.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:08.960
Help him or her to shine in
front of his or her leaders.

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.900
By this, you will not be
perceived as self-oriented.

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:13.790
This is really one of the best ways

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:15.670
just to build these relationships

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:17.010
and create these situations

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:18.590
where after you've done
something like this

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:20.920
a couple of times then
the client at some point

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:23.090
will also be willing
and happy to potentially

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:25.460
also help you out when
you really need something.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:26.680
Maybe it will be at some point

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:29.310
where you very urgently need
something from that person.

00:09:29.310 --> 00:09:31.400
And then often this person
will also be willing

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:33.880
just to sit there late
at night to create this

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:35.337
on the same day, just to help you.

00:09:35.337 --> 00:09:37.630
And of course, just the
opposite might be true

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:41.230
if you're not able to build
this trusting relationship.

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:43.741
So I hope you found these tips insightful.

